<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/create_branch.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="sql-createbranch">
 <indexterm zone="sql-createbranch">
  <primary>CREATE BRANCH</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE BRANCH</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE BRANCH</refname>
  <refpurpose>create a database branch from an existing database</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE BRANCH <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable>
    FROM DATABASE <replaceable class="parameter">source_database</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE BRANCH</command> creates a connectable database by cloning
   an existing source database and replaying source database WAL records needed
   to make the branch consistent.
  </para>

  <para>
   The source database is locked while PostgreSQL waits briefly for existing
   table DDL such as <command>CREATE TABLE</command>,
   <command>ALTER TABLE</command>, <command>CREATE INDEX</command>,
   <command>DROP TABLE</command>, and <command>TRUNCATE</command>, and
   maintenance writes such as <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</command>,
   <command>VACUUM</command>, <command>CLUSTER</command>, and
   <command>REINDEX</command> to drain, pins WAL, and clones the source
   storage.  Existing idle or read-only sessions can stay connected.  WAL
   replay then runs against the branch storage after that source lock is
   released.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The name of the branch database to create.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><replaceable class="parameter">source_database</replaceable></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The database whose storage and WAL history are used as the branch
       source.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE BRANCH</command> cannot be executed inside a transaction
   block.
  </para>

  <para>
   To create a branch, you must be a superuser or have the
   <literal>CREATEDB</literal> privilege.  The user must own the source
   database unless the source is marked as a template database.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE BRANCH</command> requires <xref linkend="guc-wal-level"/>
   to be <literal>replica</literal> or higher and
   <xref linkend="guc-max-replication-slots"/> to be greater than zero.
  </para>

  <para>
   The source database's recorded collation version must match the actual
   collation version reported by the current operating system or provider.
  </para>

  <para>
   The source database must not contain logical replication subscriptions.
   Subscriptions may hold resources outside the source database, so this
   prototype refuses to clone them into a branch.
  </para>

  <para>
   The current implementation gates writers while the source database is
   cloned.  Existing idle or read-only sessions can stay connected, but new
   source connections and write transactions wait until the branch setup
   releases the source lock.
  </para>

  <para>
   Temporary relation files that belong to live source sessions are not copied
   into the branch storage.
   Source relation cache initialization files are also skipped and rebuilt by
   branch backends.
  </para>

  <para>
   WAL replay currently supports records whose block references are local to
   the source database.  If a required WAL record also references another
   database or shared state, branch creation fails and records the failure in
   the diagnostic state file.
  </para>

  <para>
   The current WAL replay matrix is intentionally small:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Source-database block records with a redo handler are remapped to the
     branch database and replayed.  The test coverage includes heap, btree,
     GIN, TOAST, partitioned tables, relation tablespaces, post-checkpoint
     DDL, full-page images, and non-full-page-image replay.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Records with no source-database block references, including other
     database records and global records, are skipped.  Source sequence WAL is
     also skipped; sequence state comes from the flushed cloned relation.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Mixed records that reference the source database and another database or
     shared state fail branch creation.  Records without a redo handler also
     fail.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   Successful branch creation is recorded in the
   <link linkend="catalog-pg-dbbranch"><structname>pg_dbbranch</structname></link>
   system catalog.  Failed attempts are left out of the catalog and recorded
   in a diagnostic state file under <filename>global</filename>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Operators can use <structname>pg_dbbranch</structname> to inspect branch
   timing and replay size after a successful branch: source blocking time,
   clone time, replay time, WAL range bytes, scanned WAL record counts,
   replayed WAL record counts, clone result, replay method, and ready time are
   stored in the catalog row.  Failed attempts keep the same diagnostic shape
   in the state file when the failure occurs after branch metadata can be
   written.
  </para>

  <para>
   If the server stops before a branch reaches <literal>READY</literal>, the
   current prototype does not automatically resume replay during restart.  It
   scans diagnostic state files after recovery, derives the branch database
   OID from a safe recorded clone path, removes stale cloned branch storage
   under <filename>base</filename> and per-database
   <filename>pg_tblspc</filename> directories, and rewrites the state as
   <literal>FAILED</literal>.  No
   <structname>pg_dbbranch</structname> catalog row is committed for the
   incomplete branch.  The diagnostic state file remains available for
   operators to identify the last durable clone path and startup cleanup
   result.
  </para>

  <para>
   This prototype does not enforce a DB Branch quota.  Storage limits,
   replication slot limits, and filesystem errors are enforced by the
   underlying system; <command>CREATE BRANCH</command> fails and records the
   failure when those limits prevent cloning, WAL pinning, or replay.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   Create a branch named <literal>test_branch</literal> from
   <literal>production</literal>:
<programlisting>
CREATE BRANCH test_branch FROM DATABASE production;
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   There is no <command>CREATE BRANCH</command> statement in the SQL standard.
  </para>
 </refsect1>
</refentry>
